  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 PM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nAfternoon conditions are quiet in the wake of the shortwave and cold \nfront, courtesy of the weak high pressure ridging extending overhead \nfrom IA/IL. Visible satellite shows a plume of wildfire smoke \nspanning the Northern Plains across IA and southern MN, but sfc \nobservations do not indicate this is currently resulting in vis \nrestrictions and significant air quality impacts. GOES WV imagery \nshows broad mid level troughing across central Canada which largely \nwill not change until mid next week, yielding quazi-zonal flow over \nthe Upper Great Lakes. This means that through early next week, \nprecip chances will be supported by diurnal instability and driven \nby transient shortwaves/frontal boundaries.\n\nThe rest of today remains dry. Highs will be more seasonable in the \n70s to low 80s under mostly sunny skies. Southwest low level flow \npoints toward lifting the aforementioned smoke into the region with \nthe HRRR smoke model suggesting this will be overhead by this \nevening. Left mention out of the gridded forecast for now given the \nHRRR model disagreement with the RAP/Canadian smoke models as well \nas no surface impacts are expected. That said, hazy skies are \npossible before cloud cover increases tonight. Mostly dry conditions \ncontinue tonight as temps settle into the 50s to low 60s, coldest in \nthe interior. Chances for showers return from the west late tonight \nas a weak frontal boundary sags south from northern Ontario \nalongside a shortwave. Limited and capped elevated instability (<500 \nJ/kg) with strong shear (~50 kts) will be present so thunder can't \nbe ruled out with this elevated convection, but strong to severe \nstorms are not expected. This initial round hangs out mostly over \nLake Superior and the Keweenaw, but isolated showers could grace the \nwest and northern shores into Sunday morning (20-30% chance). PoPs \nexpand to the rest of the UP the remainder of Sunday as diurnal \ninstability builds, the front continues to sag over the CWA, and a \nupper level jet streak passes overhead. Instability will be run of \nthe mill for meager summertime convection (mean values well below \n1000 J/kg, 90th percentile reaches near 1000 J/kg over the west and \nsouth-central) with continued strong shear up to 40 kts. Given \nlackluster moisture and forcing, severe storms are not expected. \nCAMs are not in great agreement on coverage and timing; some of \nwhich have little to any precip whatsoever (HRRR). Unsurprisingly, \nprecip probabilities reflect this unimpressive set up with only 20-\n50% chances for amounts exceeding 0.25 inches. \n\nDry weather returns for Sunday night and Monday. Lows dip into \nnormal temps in the upper 40s to upper 50s. The rest of the forecast \nsees a slow trend to slightly cooler than normal temps as highs \ntrend toward upper 60s to mid 70s for the end of the work week with \nlows in the 40s to mid 50s. Shortwaves Tuesday and Wednesday bring \nlow chances for showers and storms (mostly 15-30% chance), then \ndrier weather is favored into the weekend as stronger high pressure \nreturns.",
